          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "**Long-term use and review.** The GERD guidance (guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features) says that where long-term acid suppression is genuinely needed, it is continued \"at the lowest effective intensity with periodic review, rather than renewed indefinitely without reassessment.\" It names erosive esophagitis, Barrett's esophagus, and symptom return when the medicine is withdrawn as cases where ongoing use may be needed. The passage does not give a set review interval, and it does not say whether long-term use is a concern for any particular person. Your prescriber is the right person to ask whether your ongoing need has been reviewed, and whether a trial of stopping or lowering the dose makes sense for you. Do not change your medicine on your own.\n\n**Coding.** In ICD-10-CM, reflux disease is category K21 (coding-gerd-k21). The one distinction it draws is whether esophagitis is present, and that depends on a finding from a scope examination, not on how severe the heartburn feels or how long the medicine has been taken. Most records fall under the branch without esophagitis.\n\n**Not covered by these sources.** The retrieved material does not describe the specific risks of long-term acid-suppressing medicine, nor a monitoring schedule for it. I cannot answer those questions from these sources.\n\n**Important:** If you have chest pain or pressure, especially with breathlessness, sweating, or activity, seek emergency care right away. The GERD guidance says chest discomfort must not be assumed to be reflux until a heart cause has been considered (guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
